Based on the SEM images, three spots were set for the EDX measurement in each iron oxide layer (see Fig.  3 ) in order to determine the chemical composition and compare it to the investigations from  Young  et al .  [ 34 ]. Using EDX measurements, no phases can be determined, only the present element distribution in at. %. In Table  3 , the measured values for three different iron oxide layers formed on the three different steels are shown. The amount of iron and oxygen is displayed in at. % as mean value over three measured spots. According to the known chemical composition Fe 2 O 3  for hematite, an average of 40 at. % for Fe and 60 at. % for O is expected. The EDX measurement corresponded well with the expected values with an average value of approx. 40.1–41.4 at. % for Fe and approx. 58.5–59.9 at. % for O. A chemical composition of 42.86 at. % for Fe and 57.14 at. % for O was expected for magnetite Fe 3 O 4 . For 45Cr4, the measured values with 42.2 at. % for Fe and 57.8 at. % for O over three measuring points match very well with the data in [ 34 ]. The EDX measurements for C45 and 45Cr3 showed an average value of approx. 44.2–45.2 at. % for Fe and approx. 54.8–55.8 at. % for O over three measuring points. The EDX measurement showed a slightly higher deviation compared to the hematite values. But the values for all three steels are still in the same range. For wustite FeO, a chemical composition of 50.0 at. % for Fe and 50.0 at. % is expected. The EDX measurement showed an average value of approx. 47.2–49.3 at. % for Fe and approx. 50.7–52.8 at. % for O over three measuring points, which reveals small deviations from the expectation and the given data in [ 34 ]. In addition to wustite, this layer also consists of small precipitates of magnetite. This aspect is analysed in more detail below. Fig. 3 The position of each measured EDX spot within the scale layers grown on C45 (A), 45Cr3 (B) and 45Cr4 (C). Red indicates hematite, yellow indicates magnetite, and purp