In order to investigate the small precipitates in the wustite layer, EDX measurements were carried out specifically on the precipitates. Figure  4  A shows an enlarged section of the wustite layer for 45Cr3. Identical structures (dark-appearing phases) were observed in the wustite matrix of the other two steels, so the 45Cr3 stands as an example for all investigated materials. The green crosses show the measuring points for the EDX analysis inside the dark phases. The determined values are listed in Table  4 . The proportions of the chemical elements (Fe and O) are given in at. % for each investigated material. The EDX measurement showed an average value of approx. 42.8–45.2 at. % for Fe and approx. 54.8–57.2 at. % for O over three measuring points. These values are in the comparable range as the magnetite values shown in Table  3 . The identification for magnetite as inclusions in the wustite layer could also be investigated by  Behrens  et al .  [ 24 ] using nanohardness measurement. Fig. 4 Position of each measured EDX spot inside the dark phase ( A ) and subscale ( B ). Green indicates the measured spots within the dark phase and blue within the subscale Table 4 The chemical composition (Fe and O) of the dark-appearing phases identified in the wustite matrix, and the chemical composition (Fe, O, Cr, Si, etc.) of the subscale in at%. is an average value obtained from three measured spots Element fraction in at. % Dark phase Subscale X ¯ \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
